- Everyone, I'm Caitlin.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.820
I'm going to be doing a little bit

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.890
of like a coding tutorial
today based in R and shiny

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.850
for those that haven't
worked with R or shiny,

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.770
we have linked on the page

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:23.890
some other videos kind of
introing R and shiny separately.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:25.990
So you do need a little bit of background

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:29.770
for those those two languages and packages

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:33.890
to kind of get a-- feel
comfortable with this video,

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:37.640
also linked a bunch of really
fabulous free R resources.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.410
If you want to really dive
into R not just for data viz

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.770
but for data science and
programming in general.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:49.500
I really wanted to focus
on kind of like a hands-on

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.670
sort of example of how you can work

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.890
with actual COVID-19 data,
that's out there and it's public.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.020
There's a lot of it out there.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.483
Not all of them are created
equal unfortunately,

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.230
some of them are so messy.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.710
It's hard to even explain how messy it is.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.110
We're going to start with a
kind of more friendly example

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.270
from the New York City Health Department,

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.703
so since I'm in New York City,

00:01:15.703 --> 00:01:17.398
Rockefeller's in New York city,

00:01:17.398 --> 00:01:21.620
this is a more personal kind
of like look at COVID-19.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.280
We're gonna start on GitHub actually

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.750
with the source of the
data I'll be using today.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.350
For those that haven't
worked with GitHub before

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.740
it's a really phenomenal kind of place

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.270
for the broader data community.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.680
So the format of this is essentially

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.660
what are called repositories
or repos are shared

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.000
and stored on here.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.850
So this is sort of the username
for the Health Department

00:01:48.898 --> 00:01:51.640
and so actually don't know

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.910
they probably have other data here.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.428
We can take a take a quick peek.

00:01:55.428 --> 00:01:57.543
Oh, it turns out it's just COVID data.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.500
Yeah. So a fun thing about COVID

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.670
for a lot of Health Departments

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:07.670
is the kind of urgent need
to share data with the public

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.850
was really fast and a
lot of Health Departments

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:15.170
had no capacity to do this in any way,

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.370
let alone just like handle
and collect the data.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.440
New York City in general
for various departments

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.790
has pretty good like open data resources,

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.960
totally recommend checking
out those data sets

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.510
because there's a lot of really fun stuff

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:29.820
you can learn about the city that way.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.510
Like COVID kind of hit everyone very fast

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.157
and the need for information

00:02:34.157 --> 00:02:36.050
and the need to talk
about that information

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.440
and then subsequently visualize
and analyze that information

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.600
was really fast and New
York City had the capacity

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.560
to ramp up, but a lot of places didn't,

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.760
and it took them a while to get to a place

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.580
where they could share data,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.420
so as a fun scavenger
hunt on your own time

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.510
you can probably try to look at data

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.290
from other state's counties

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.750
to get a sense of the
variability of how this looks,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.450
this is the repository we're
going to work with today.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:03.540
There's a lot in here.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.880
We're not mapping every
single thing on here.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:10.880
The game plan will be to
create a map of the city

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:16.410
and broken down by a
sort of form of zip code

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.260
which we'll talk about in a bit,

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.407
on top of that map obviously
we care about COVID data

00:03:21.407 --> 00:03:22.250
and the spread of COVID-19.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.250
So we'll be looking at
a few different metrics

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.570
the kind of most basic
one being case rate.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:33.520
So cases appearing per a
certain number of people

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.400
in the population.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.190
Take a minute to look at the data itself

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:42.150
and what it is, I talked
a lot in the last video

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.380
about how you really have to
be familiar with your data

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.150
before you even begin to
think about visualizing it.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:48.410
The NYC Health Department,

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.910
has their own set of
visualizations actually

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.300
on their data webpage.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.100
We're going to make
something similar to that,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:59.050
but we'll be focusing mostly
on just a handful of metrics.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.640
Whereas I think they're
showing a bit more.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:01.473
Already you can see,

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:03.490
and this is mirrored
on the GitHub as well,

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.840
how they're defining cases.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.450
What is a confirmed case?

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.560
People with a positive molecular test,

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:11.860
probable cases people with
a positive antigen test.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.030
So the right, there are different ways

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.770
in which we're assessing someone
having or not having COVID.

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:17.900
You guys are probably familiar

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.230
with many different types
of tests that are available,

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.900
ones that are detecting
the presence of an antigen

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.050
ones that are based on PCR.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.000
There are many different ways

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.990
in which you can actually detect COVID-19.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.190
And because of that, there are
many ways to actually define

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.700
someone having a case or not.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.730
So we're going to make a
kind of version of this.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:42.460
So this is a static map what's
known as a Chloropeth map.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:47.040
So that just means that
regions are sort of colored

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.050
by a data point.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.890
In this case, it's a seven
day percent positive.

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:52.830
So what does that mean?

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.700
Percent of people that had
a test who tested positive

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.390
that's actually quite
strict definition, right?

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.980
This is not like the percent
of the full population

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:02.040
that tested positive.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.810
This is the percent of the people

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.820
that actually had tests
that tested positive.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.753
And they have this really important,

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.330
note here that all data are incomplete

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.530
because we're not testing
every single person.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.820
And the other feature
here, this is a seven day.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:17.410
So what does that mean?

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.430
Seven day means that,

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.950
these numbers are being
aggregated on a weekly basis.

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.570
Individual days can really
mess up data collection.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.330
And so to kind of correct for that noise

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.200
or kind of attribute
too much interpretation

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.580
or weight to one given day,

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:33.960
you average across seven days

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.090
to calculate a percent positivity rate,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.850
you can imagine if this was
a daily percent positive

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.390
let's say, I don't know,

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.350
somewhere down here in
the lower East side,

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.590
one of this zip codes

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:53.620
maybe three days of data were input

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:56.510
or collected for some reason on one day

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.730
instead of, you know split
across those three days.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.840
If someone were looking on
one day from the day before,

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.100
and like coming back to this map

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.470
and seeing all of a sudden

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.030
this becomes a much, much darker color.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:09.230
They can interpret thinking like,

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:13.090
oh my God something happened
on this day to cause you know,

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.770
some huge burst in percent positivity

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.080
which wouldn't be the truth, right?

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:21.223
That's like a slower division of time.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.110
So kind of drop in all those new cases.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.990
So this is why we have the weekly averages

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.340
or aggregations I should
say of percent positive.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:32.370
It's more stable and reflects
kind of change happening

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:33.220
in real time.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:35.050
Some people actually
even extend this further

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:39.610
to further reduce the
kind of potential noise.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.150
So like 14 day, for example

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.880
we just had the holidays in December.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.470
And so that was actually
like a huge problem

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.590
with reporting in a lot of states counties

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.810
the weekly averages were
actually susceptible to that,

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:56.290
because there was this kind
of longish period of time

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:59.000
for some people need more
than a week of vacation

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.480
and people weren't getting tested.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.340
There were backlogs from
on the reporting end.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.110
There were these kind of
artificial drops in numbers,

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.717
which you know people
had to sort of clarify

00:07:09.717 --> 00:07:13.280
for their audiences through our
publishing data around this.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.310
Hopefully they did, some
of them I'm sure did not.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.210
This is kind of a map sort
of what we're going to do

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:19.440
but we're actually going to do this,

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.120
over the course of several months.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.200
So starting in August
this seven day numbers

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.370
this calculation,

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:29.460
didn't start happening
actually in New York City

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.780
until early August.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.510
Jumping back over to the GitHub repo.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:36.310
If you look at any GitHub repository

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:42.120
you often will encounter
a readme mark down page

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.350
that's what M D stands for.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:48.210
This is pretty awesome in
terms of how well explained

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.830
the data is, it even goes
into detail about like

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:52.290
how to download the data,

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.430
like how to make requests
if you have questions,

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:56.200
you can submit an issue on GitHub

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.720
also updates to the
way in which maybe data

00:07:58.720 --> 00:07:59.810
is being described

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:03.020
or the new types of metrics
that are being included

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.170
at different points.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:05.560
'Cause these things happen.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:06.393
For example,

00:08:06.393 --> 00:08:08.653
they change from PCR test
to molecular test, right?

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:11.270
This is a technical distinction,

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:12.720
but they get into this stuff.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.390
There are so many different kind of facets

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:18.540
to talking about COVID and
they really do a nice job here

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.220
of explaining the data.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.320
On your own time you
can definitely explore

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.360
the different types of data here

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.640
but we're going to focus on trends

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.560
since we're going to look over
the course of several months.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.913
And so in this folder,
we have lots of CSVs.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.710
So that's, comma-separated.
The department recommends

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.500
against interpreting daily changes

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.290
as one day's worth of data,

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:43.330
due to the difference between date
of event and date of report.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:44.680
So that's really key too.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:48.090
It's something I didn't actually
mention of people associating

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.260
an event being recorded on a given day

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.180
as it actually happening on that day,

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.930
but think about in real life,
if you've tested for COVID

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:56.920
or have had COVID

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:00.310
and that testing event
could also be lagged

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.970
between when it's actually
reported to the system.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.630
There's not this kind of
like instantaneous thing

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:07.050
where you get a positive
test in some systems,

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.970
somewhere on a server it gets updated

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:12.080
to indicate your positive COVID case.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.150
So that's an inherent flaw

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:18.360
with what these types
of reporting weekly data

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.450
so we're going to be focusing on.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.290
And so there's a lot of data in here,

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.150
antibody testing, case rate,

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:27.550
which is one of the ones
we're going to look at,

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.350
again, the cases by day

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:32.773
which has all of these different metrics.

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:35.547
And these kind of prefixes here

00:09:35.547 --> 00:09:37.860
are actually sorted by borough.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.220
So people wanted to
compare for New York City,

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.010
you know like the Bronx
versus Manhattan versus Queens

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:44.313
versus Staten Island.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:46.700
These were important

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:50.010
especially kind of getting
broader area aggregations

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:52.530
of hotspots and things like that.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.900
Deaths by day. Hospitalizations by day.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.330
Percent positive again, is
when we're going to use.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.960
And then we're also
going to take test rate.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:05.760
If you notice the three data
points that I'm mentioning

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.550
are ones that are followed by MODZCTA.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:13.150
So ZCTA is a specific
type of geography, right?

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:17.560
So we can talk about across the city.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.710
We can talk about borough
or should I mentioned before

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:21.620
so it's like kind of sub
regions within the city

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:23.350
but then we can talk about zip codes.

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:27.813
MODZCTA actually is not exactly zip codes

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:30.850
which I'm going to talk about in a bit

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:35.850
but it's a way to map
what are regions defined

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.760
by different zip codes.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.320
So it's much smaller region

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:46.060
to get a sense of COVID spread
in those smaller regions.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:49.260
So much like this map, right?

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.700
These are this MODZCTA
regions I was talking about.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:54.670
This is much better than
just kind of comparing

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:55.503
the full borough.

00:10:55.503 --> 00:10:56.336
Why?

00:10:56.336 --> 00:10:58.840
All for those that know their
boroughs in New York City,

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:02.720
you can look within these big boroughs

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.950
and you can see that there are actually

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:09.750
lot of variability across
that even adjacent zip codes.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:14.750
So that is actually really
important to inform a public

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:17.680
that is concerned about travel

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:22.680
or concerned about quarantines
and transmission of the virus

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:24.930
and all of this stuff, right?

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:26.950
So people wanting to be informed

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:29.360
about severity of the disease.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:33.310
And this is also helpful for
people on the government side

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:34.300
who are trying to figure out,

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:36.620
you know where to prioritize resources,

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:41.120
if COVID is more severely spreading

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.360
in one region versus another.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.470
This also affects hospitals too, right?

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:49.710
Hospitals being either
distributed evenly across the city

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:51.160
or not which is definitely not the case

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:55.180
but you know, if a hospital is here

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:57.270
it's gonna need more resources probably

00:11:57.270 --> 00:11:59.040
than a hospital here.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.420
So that is why we're looking

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:05.120
at these sort of smaller regions.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:08.610
Again, I'm sort of, you know,
filtering and making decisions

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.260
about the visit we're going to make today

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:12.140
since this is more of a tutorial

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:13.720
but there's a lot of data here.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:15.800
And so you don't have to
do exactly what I'm doing.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.210
And this is meant to just sort of teach

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:20.400
how to make a specific
type of visualization.

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:21.710
It's going to be interactive as well too.

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.250
So this one, when you hover over

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:27.570
you get some information
about each zip code

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:30.000
whatever you do, if you're
working with this data

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:30.833
if you're working with a different dataset

00:12:30.833 --> 00:12:33.640
make sure you're
understanding what the data is.

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:35.440
This is super detailed.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:38.900
We're doing the seven day
aggregations as well too.

