00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:11.920
So let's talk about what MODZCTA is,

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:14.060
because it's a little confusing

00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:16.230
again, if we need you have
a question about the data

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:19.670
cutting into the actual
readme for the GitHub repose

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:20.960
is a really good place to start.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:25.430
The data here is accompanied
by this lovely explanation.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:30.020
So MODZCTA stands for Modified
Zip Code Tabulation Area

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:32.290
and ZCTA is just Zip Code Tabulation Area

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:34.500
because the zip code,
as it's explained here

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.810
isn't actually a domain.

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:39.300
It's not like a county or a state.

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:42.480
It's actually a collection of points

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:44.210
that make up a mail delivery route

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:46.230
which is not something
we typically think about.

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:48.520
But when we're talking
about regions and space,

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:52.880
and so when when we're
dealing with mapping in R,

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:55.640
really any software mapping for viz,

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:57.210
we need to actually understand

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:59.770
what the regions are bound by.

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:02.170
There are these issues as well too with,

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:06.080
you know, some areas
like having their own,

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:07.670
buildings having their own zip code,

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:09.320
or weird stuff like that.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:10.620
Essentially what the
Health Department did,

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:13.380
is create modifications to these ZCTAs

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:16.400
that take these like tiny little zip codes

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:19.110
that are affiliated with like
maybe a building or something

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:22.700
and combine them into like a
slightly bigger region, right?

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:24.740
To make these estimates
for population size,

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:28.920
bore legitimate, because
dividing by a hundred thousand,

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:31.970
you know, is a good
normalization across the board,

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:36.160
but doing it for a place
that has a tiny population

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:37.590
it might not actually capture

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:40.720
the regional spread of a disease.

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:43.400
So you want to keep your
geographies like fairly comparable

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:44.390
in terms of size,

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:49.130
a little bit of aggressive
detail for mapping,

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:50.480
but this is the kind of thing you run into

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:52.700
especially when you're dealing
with geographical data.

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:57.700
So to deal with this they offer
this ZCTA to MODZCTA file.

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:00.320
Just take a quick look at that.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:01.300
And you'll notice for the most part it

00:23:01.300 --> 00:23:02.420
it's like a one-to-one mapping.

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:05.360
It's like, oh, why did they
even bother doing this?

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:06.530
It's not all zip codes, right?

00:23:06.530 --> 00:23:09.040
This is pretty much uniform for everything

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:11.770
until you get down here, look at this.

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:14.890
So the MODZCTA is on this side.

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:17.470
This one MODZCTA is encapsulating

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:19.410
several different zip codes

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:21.630
because those are like
tiny, tiny little zip codes

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:25.460
not comparable to maybe a
more standardized zip code

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:26.730
the way we're going to handle this,

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:29.760
is we're going to we're
going to use the MODZCTAs.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:32.610
But if you wanted to as well

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:34.460
to make a more enhanced visualization

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:36.680
you can create like a mapping system

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:39.130
within to convert over to ZCTA.

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:43.090
So a little added complexity,
the way we're going to do this

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:46.620
in our final app is to include this table

