Why is that?

00:25:31.963 --> 00:25:36.963
So currently we want to
map by zip code or ZCTA,

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:38.313
I should say.

00:25:38.313 --> 00:25:40.430
But right now that zip
code data is kind of spread

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:41.900
across this data of frame

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:45.260
in a classic data cleaning situation

00:25:45.260 --> 00:25:47.130
or should I say data wrangling.

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:51.030
We're going to be shifting
the format of this data frame

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:52.310
into a long format.

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:54.470
And what does that mean instead of having,

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:56.761
you know, all of these
variables spread out

00:25:56.761 --> 00:25:58.550
in this wide format,

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:02.120
where for every week we
have a different zip codes

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:03.440
going across this way.

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:04.610
We want to have a situation

00:26:04.610 --> 00:26:08.550
where we have a column that is zip code

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:13.010
and the case rates are
aggregated kind of lengthwise

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:14.920
as opposed to across
the zip codes this way.

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:16.050
But this will be,

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:19.880
you know, a few columns
as opposed to 22 columns.

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:22.360
The first thing we're going
to do is actually remove,

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:24.240
we're not interested in the borough data

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:25.160
or the city-wide data.

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:27.280
So we're going to remove
these first few columns

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:28.190
which does that.

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:29.540
I'm calling it case rates now

00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:31.750
just to give it a new variable name.

00:26:31.750 --> 00:26:34.200
So let's take a look at what
that is after I do that.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:38.290
So now we've got rid of that
stuff that was here, right?

00:26:38.290 --> 00:26:40.700
The city-wide, the borough-wide

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:42.460
and now we just have zip code data.

00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:44.860
Awesome, this is the reshaping.

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:48.290
So tidyverse introduced
actually very recently

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:50.830
this new function, a new set of functions

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:52.130
pivot wider and pivot longer.

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:53.770
It used to be gather and spread.

00:26:53.770 --> 00:26:55.530
I think this is a little
bit more intuitive

00:26:55.530 --> 00:26:56.990
that the new language,

00:26:56.990 --> 00:26:58.280
but essentially what I'm doing right

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:00.930
is I'm moving into the long form format.

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:02.190
So pivoting it longer.

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:06.460
I'm taking all of these
columns 178 columns.

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:09.483
Actually, I think I said
22 before that was wrong.

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:13.640
170 columns, 22 rows and
we're going to put them all

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:16.400
actually now into one
column called MODZCTA.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:19.250
The prefects for that is going to be,

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:22.140
that case rate blank, right?

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:23.040
So that's the prefix

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:27.730
that's encapsulating all
the zip code data I want

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:29.550
and that's going to be
kind of removed, right?

00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:32.690
We don't want a list that
