00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:02.967
So that's going to like
orient the polygons

00:39:02.967 --> 00:39:04.980
the specific way, a lot to explain here,

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:07.827
but the CRS is thing of
like a coordinate system

00:39:07.827 --> 00:39:10.840
and we're transforming
R current geography data

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:12.500
into this sort of format.

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:14.110
And then we're going to
run the leaflet function.

00:39:14.110 --> 00:39:16.900
So taking that data and running leaflet

00:39:16.900 --> 00:39:19.390
that's enough to sort
of like generate a map,

00:39:19.390 --> 00:39:22.380
but we have to sort of then set
the options within that map.

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:26.580
And so to do so you can
add layers called tiles

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:29.000
or in this case provider tiles.

00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:32.260
And this is really like the
type of map you're using.

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:35.950
So I'm choosing the one
that's called CartoDB.Positron

00:39:35.950 --> 00:39:38.240
but this can be a lot of things.

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:40.080
So just to show you an example

00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:43.090
this one is going to run this part.

00:39:43.090 --> 00:39:48.090
Oops, you get this warning message,

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:51.390
but you can, you can
pretty much ignore that.

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:55.580
And so I haven't put any data on here yet

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:58.760
because I haven't actually
defined anything about it.

00:39:58.760 --> 00:39:59.917
So that's why you don't
see any data on here.

00:39:59.917 --> 00:40:04.030
And all we have is this leaflet
map using open street map

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:07.800
and it's the entire world.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:08.633
So we're not even looking
at New City at this point

00:40:08.633 --> 00:40:12.410
but this is sort of what that
leaflet instance generates.

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:15.740
And the CartoDB.Positron
is this type of map.

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:17.580
You can send you to change
the way the map looks.

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:20.423
So like different types of
projections you can play with.

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:24.200
If you check as always,

00:40:24.200 --> 00:40:26.950
if you don't know what a function does,

00:40:26.950 --> 00:40:28.840
checking the help section.

00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:30.530
And so for a no map provider,

00:40:30.530 --> 00:40:32.550
there are lots of types of maps out there

00:40:32.550 --> 00:40:33.440
that you can play with.

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:38.120
So the actual help guide
example is stamen.watercolor.

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:39.890
And so let's just pop that in here

00:40:39.890 --> 00:40:42.790
and see what it does just as
give you a sense of the range

00:40:45.980 --> 00:40:50.947
stamen.watercolor I'm gonna run this.

00:40:57.430 --> 00:41:00.805
And I got this crazy surreal stream

00:41:00.805 --> 00:41:02.720
but this is cool, right?

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:06.150
You can imagine whole types of maps.

00:41:06.150 --> 00:41:08.780
There are a lot of types you
can actually grab in here

00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:10.960
different map providers,
interface with leaflet

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:14.010
and offer these up as
packages you can play with.

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:15.100
So I think that's pretty cool,

00:41:15.100 --> 00:41:17.960
but if you're a member like
we think that this data viz

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:20.500
in the purpose of it, we're not trying to,

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:22.490
you know, showcase some awesome art,

00:41:22.490 --> 00:41:24.000
even though it maybe in another situation

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:25.770
that might be what we're
trying to accomplish.

00:41:25.770 --> 00:41:29.120
This is a very cool map,
but at the same time

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:33.120
we are using color in
this case to highlight

00:41:33.120 --> 00:41:34.230
the data we care about.

00:41:34.230 --> 00:41:37.660
So in this case, we're mapping
case rate in New York City.

00:41:37.660 --> 00:41:39.560
And so we actually want the cases

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:40.720
to be the thing that's colored

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:43.050
and that's what the eye
is going to be drawn to.

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:44.460
As you load them, you can play with them

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:46.790
and see you can zoom in on locations.

00:41:46.790 --> 00:41:48.740
You're getting all kinds of stuff here.

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:51.870
Oh boy, it's actually quite beautiful.

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:54.800
You can play with this and
look at the different maps

00:41:54.800 --> 00:41:57.440
and see how they look
at different resolutions

00:41:57.440 --> 00:41:58.840
and zoom settings.

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:02.750
Let me reset the map to go back to,

00:42:02.750 --> 00:42:03.860
it looks like fairly boring

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:06.460
where we're going to put
some pretty stuff on there.

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:14.690
So again, just running that
and now we get to the data.

00:42:15.127 --> 00:42:18.080
And so this is a lot,
there's a lot happening here.

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:20.800
We're gonna to walk through this slowly.

00:42:20.800 --> 00:42:22.970
So now we're in the add polygon section.

00:42:22.970 --> 00:42:26.220
There are lots of things you
can add to a leaflet bat.

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:28.010
You can add circle markers.

00:42:28.010 --> 00:42:32.720
You can add kind of like pop up points.

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:37.150
We're working with polygons
because the shape file.

00:42:37.150 --> 00:42:38.150
That's why we went through all that work

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:41.490
of joining in that geography
data are considered polygon

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:42.800
sort of like these irregular shapes.

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:45.830
If you wanted to add sort
of standard like little dots

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:50.160
or circles or some sort of like
uniform thing across there,

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:52.220
there's a leaflet function for that.

00:42:52.220 --> 00:42:54.100
But in this case, we're adding polygons

00:42:54.100 --> 00:42:58.140
because we have very specific geometries.

00:42:58.140 --> 00:42:59.910
Those are those shape file data

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:03.770
showing those MODZCTA regions.

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:05.390
So we're using add polygons.

00:43:05.390 --> 00:43:06.390
And within this function,

00:43:06.390 --> 00:43:09.410
there are a lot of arguments
that you can play with.

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:11.340
So the very first one
is we want to make sure

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:14.800
we incorporate our labels
that we wrote up over here.

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:16.040
So this is what's going to happen

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:19.420
when we hover over any given polygon.

00:43:19.420 --> 00:43:21.460
So label equals labels.

00:43:21.460 --> 00:43:22.720
That's labels up here.

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:23.840
Some of the design factors

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:25.950
these are really not a hard and fast.

00:43:25.950 --> 00:43:27.524
These are just things you can play with.

00:43:27.524 --> 00:43:32.524
So the stroke of the actual polygon shape

00:43:34.038 --> 00:43:35.120
I'm just turning that off.

00:43:35.120 --> 00:43:36.930
Smoothing just a little bit soothing

00:43:36.930 --> 00:43:37.890
because some of the edges

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:42.347
are look a little bit kind
of not super straight.

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:46.810
And so I just upped it up to 2.5 a bit,

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:49.110
just to add a little bit of soothing.

00:43:49.110 --> 00:43:52.410
So the lines aren't super
kind of jagged aesthetically

00:43:52.410 --> 00:43:53.390
it just looks a little bit nicer.

00:43:53.390 --> 00:43:56.060
It doesn't change anything
about the polygon itself

00:43:56.060 --> 00:43:59.383
opacity being one, fill opacity being 0.7,

00:44:00.758 --> 00:44:02.510
and so these are two
different features, right?

00:44:02.510 --> 00:44:05.920
So the opacity of the
kind of polygon outline

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:07.190
and then fill opacity

00:44:07.190 --> 00:44:08.877
is like what's inside the polygon itself.

00:44:08.877 --> 00:44:11.230
And then the most important
part here actually

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:13.010
is ends up being fill color

00:44:13.010 --> 00:44:17.030
because we're trying to make
essentially a Chloropeth map.

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:18.210
What that is again,

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:22.670
is recoloring regions
based on a data metric.

00:44:22.670 --> 00:44:25.403
So in this case, it's case rate
but it's not just case rate

00:44:25.403 --> 00:44:28.530
because we want to apply
that color function

00:44:28.530 --> 00:44:30.000
we defined up here.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.020
So for all case rate values

00:44:32.020 --> 00:44:35.170
we want to assign a
specific color on the scale

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:36.200
that we set up.

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:39.820
And that scale is going from
this orange to red colors,

00:44:39.820 --> 00:44:40.653
sort of like a light,

00:44:40.653 --> 00:44:42.550
it looks actually a
little more like yellowy

00:44:43.825 --> 00:44:45.720
the light yellow color to a darker red.

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:47.600
And so this is the notation for that.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:49.870
This is the actually function
notation I should say.

00:44:49.870 --> 00:44:51.500
And so the fill color

00:44:51.500 --> 00:44:53.600
and so the polygon will be filled in

00:44:53.600 --> 00:44:55.810
according to the case rate there,

00:44:55.810 --> 00:44:57.580
the other option again,
this is another place

00:44:57.580 --> 00:44:59.140
where you can fiddle around with

00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:02.271
and see how the different
features affect the shape

00:45:02.271 --> 00:45:05.440
and look, but we're adding
in some highlight options

00:45:05.440 --> 00:45:09.000
because we actually want
to sort of single out

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:10.260
the polygon you're hovering over.

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:12.930
So thinking for the user,
using the interactive map

00:45:12.930 --> 00:45:14.690
if you're hovering over region,

00:45:14.690 --> 00:45:16.490
you want there to be a
little bit of feedback

00:45:16.490 --> 00:45:18.500
being like okay this is the
one you're hovering over.

00:45:18.500 --> 00:45:22.000
So instead of, you know,
just to set up polygons

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:24.230
they're colored in a sort of static way

00:45:24.230 --> 00:45:25.910
but they don't actually change

00:45:25.910 --> 00:45:28.740
any sort of color when
you hover over them.

00:45:28.740 --> 00:45:31.660
So this is just gives the
user a bit more confidence

00:45:31.660 --> 00:45:33.010
they're hovering over the right thing

00:45:33.010 --> 00:45:34.740
and know what they're hovering over.

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:36.160
So adding in a very light,

00:45:36.160 --> 00:45:39.070
it's kind of like a gray
scale highlight option.

00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:42.100
And this is that you bring
to front is important here

00:45:42.100 --> 00:45:44.350
so that you actually see
that when you hover over

00:45:44.350 --> 00:45:46.100
and then the fill is actually quite

00:45:47.210 --> 00:45:48.810
it's almost totally filled out.

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:50.620
So, you know, you hover over it.

00:45:50.620 --> 00:45:52.370
It turns like a little bit gray whitish,

00:45:52.370 --> 00:45:54.790
and then it, you move on to another one.

00:45:54.790 --> 00:45:56.350
It returns to its original color

00:45:56.350 --> 00:45:59.130
already here there's a
huge range of possibilities

00:45:59.130 --> 00:46:00.370
with what you can play with.

00:46:00.370 --> 00:46:01.730
Again, I'm going to keep saying this

00:46:01.730 --> 00:46:03.530
but it's important when making data viz

00:46:03.530 --> 00:46:06.130
have a lot of kind of like tinkering

00:46:06.130 --> 00:46:08.490
and it's kind of like
photo editing sometimes

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:10.090
when you are just playing around

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:13.040
with different aesthetic
features but in this case, right?

00:46:13.040 --> 00:46:15.200
Like I'm making choices

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:17.500
based on sort of like my
aesthetic preferences.

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:20.300
Some of those have to
do with the data itself.

00:46:20.300 --> 00:46:22.800
I want to highlight certain
features about the data.

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:24.880
Like I mentioned about
the color, for example

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:27.350
but this is not the one
right way to do this.

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:30.850
And also it's not the one right
data to actually play with.

00:46:30.850 --> 00:46:32.810
So there's a lot of stuff

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:34.960
you can visualize from this dataset.

00:46:34.960 --> 00:46:39.960
If you have questions or you
don't know what's the right way

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:42.540
to visualize something,
there are a lot of people

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:46.230
you can chat with in
different forum online.

00:46:46.230 --> 00:46:47.530
And then also you can
always reach out to me

00:46:47.530 --> 00:46:49.520
if you have questions about stuff too,

00:46:49.520 --> 00:46:52.400
there is no one right data
viz is for something, right?

00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:54.760
There are some best practices
which we've talked about,

00:46:54.760 --> 00:46:56.890
but a lot of this is just getting a feel

00:46:56.890 --> 00:46:59.810
for how to make your own visualization.

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:03.300
So I don't feel like you
need to stick with exactly

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:05.690
what I'm doing and feel
free to experiment.

00:47:05.690 --> 00:47:07.300
'Cause that's where it gets fun.

00:47:07.300 --> 00:47:08.230
And the last thing we're going to do

00:47:08.230 --> 00:47:09.960
in this leaflet function.

00:47:09.960 --> 00:47:13.440
So again, every single one
of these is a pipe function.

00:47:13.440 --> 00:47:15.630
So add provider tiles add polygons

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:19.080
is very kind of deep outlier format.

00:47:19.080 --> 00:47:21.500
So we've added our polygons

00:47:21.500 --> 00:47:23.340
and then we're gonna add our legend

00:47:23.340 --> 00:47:27.230
and we're going to put this
position on the bottom, right?

00:47:27.230 --> 00:47:28.550
I'm giving it some opacity.

00:47:28.550 --> 00:47:30.870
So it looks, it's hovering
a bit over the map.

00:47:30.870 --> 00:47:34.330
And then again, we have
to match our color scheme.

00:47:34.330 --> 00:47:36.590
So that pal function we defined before.

00:47:36.590 --> 00:47:40.150
Same thing up here, values
again, being case rate

00:47:40.150 --> 00:47:42.800
and giving the legend
at title as well too.

00:47:42.800 --> 00:47:45.463
So let's run this whole thing.

00:47:46.472 --> 00:47:49.680
And so you'll see sort
of how the code lines up

00:47:49.680 --> 00:47:51.160
with what we see.

00:47:51.160 --> 00:47:56.160
I can run this map interactive
is the one we just made.

00:47:56.700 --> 00:47:58.580
One thing you'll probably remember

00:47:58.580 --> 00:48:03.580
is that our big data frame the all MODZCTA

00:48:04.600 --> 00:48:06.370
had a lot of weeks in it.

00:48:06.370 --> 00:48:09.910
We went to long, a lot of work

00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:11.640
to get all those weeks out there.

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:13.520
This doesn't actually specify a week,

00:48:13.520 --> 00:48:15.170
if you notice in the code

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:16.950
I'm not actually pointing
it to a specific week.

00:48:16.950 --> 00:48:20.560
So what this is doing is just
taking one snapshot of it

00:48:20.560 --> 00:48:22.397
and kind of stacking all the
polygons on top of each other.

00:48:22.397 --> 00:48:24.800
That's sort of why it
took a minute to load

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:26.580
which is not what we
want in the final product

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:29.220
but just for visualization
of kind of the aesthetics

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:30.510
of the whole piece,

00:48:30.510 --> 00:48:32.490
this is what our kind of final product

00:48:32.490 --> 00:48:34.430
will roughly look like restaurant.

00:48:34.430 --> 00:48:37.130
Imagine as a rough draft,
this is an interactive map.

00:48:37.130 --> 00:48:38.500
So I can, you know sort of play around

00:48:38.500 --> 00:48:42.203
and drag zoom in, zoom out, zoom in.

00:48:43.150 --> 00:48:44.970
There's a lot of data on this actually,

00:48:44.970 --> 00:48:47.640
you can see the structure
of the different boroughs

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:49.900
and this is colored in based on case rate.

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:51.050
As you can see, right?

00:48:51.050 --> 00:48:52.430
Every time you hover over something,

00:48:52.430 --> 00:48:55.990
you get a little bit of
feedback here from the coloring

00:48:55.990 --> 00:48:57.820
and you also get that pop up information

00:48:57.820 --> 00:48:58.840
that we work to construct.

00:48:58.840 --> 00:49:00.120
So you have the zip code

00:49:00.120 --> 00:49:03.160
and then you also have the number of cases

00:49:03.160 --> 00:49:04.630
per a hundred thousand people.

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:05.463
And this is nice

00:49:05.463 --> 00:49:07.720
because you have that backdrop
with the information about,

00:49:07.720 --> 00:49:11.202
you know, versus from open
street map is just the actual,

00:49:11.202 --> 00:49:15.630
you know, city itself is behind it

00:49:17.360 --> 00:49:21.300
but you then also have
your polygon stacked on top

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:24.160
and the color we mapped
all possible cases.

00:49:24.160 --> 00:49:26.960
So our highest possible case number

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:30.933
was in like 1400 something.

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:34.630
And so that's in this top range.

00:49:34.630 --> 00:49:37.570
We set as a sort of auto
cut the full spectrum

00:49:37.570 --> 00:49:41.360
into nine evenly created bins.

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:44.910
And so that's why we
have these 200 size bins

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:46.000
but of course, right?

00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:47.160
Like you can play with this.

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:51.830
You can set it such that each
bin is a very defined number

00:49:51.830 --> 00:49:53.150
and the bins are like, I don't know,

00:49:53.150 --> 00:49:55.480
zero to 50 then 50 to a hundred.

00:49:55.480 --> 00:49:56.313
If you do that, though, you have to,

00:49:56.313 --> 00:49:57.810
you know remind the viewer

00:49:57.810 --> 00:50:00.290
that the bins are not equally sized.

00:50:00.290 --> 00:50:01.480
You might want to do that in this case

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:04.380
just because of how the bins or excuse me

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:06.720
how the cases are case
numbers are distributed.

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:08.290
But in this case, it's as simple

00:50:08.290 --> 00:50:12.030
to just cut it automatically
into this number.

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:14.120
Especially with cases on the rise

00:50:14.120 --> 00:50:16.310
addressing the y-axis with cases rising

00:50:16.310 --> 00:50:17.610
is a kind of depressing thing to do,

00:50:17.610 --> 00:50:19.920
but you have to always
check to make sure your data

00:50:19.920 --> 00:50:22.150
is actually within the
scope of data color.

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:26.060
Okay? So now that we
have our interactive app

00:50:27.225 --> 00:50:29.050
the kind of standalone leaflet.

00:50:29.050 --> 00:50:31.940
You can save it using
save widget function.

00:50:31.940 --> 00:50:33.203
So doing this.

00:50:33.203 --> 00:50:34.960
This is the object name.

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:36.830
So this one's called map interactive

00:50:36.830 --> 00:50:38.730
and then you give it a actual file name.

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:40.480
So was an actual HTML file.

00:50:40.480 --> 00:50:43.840
You can then load this up wherever.

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:47.220
So this HTML file, you
can drag into any browser

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:49.320
loaded up there, and
it's an interactive map.

00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:51.540
You can scroll around
it and view and share,

00:50:51.540 --> 00:50:52.450
which is really cool.

00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:54.903
So this is how you would
